PIPELINE EVIDENCE:

The correction was timestamped 19:15:00 UTC. The standards review ran at 19:18:27 UTC â€” after the correction was logged. The standards layer produced a `standards-cleared` finding named `attention-preserve-correction` with message "hidden correction state corrected," which indicates it detected the existence of a correction record but did not enforce the correction's `must_not_include` body constraint against the actual body text. The standards gate returned `publishable: true` despite the body violating the explicit `must_not_include` requirement. The root failure is in the correction-application step: the correction metadata was written but the body URLs were not updated before the manuscript moved forward.

---

PIPELINE IMPROVEMENT:

The correction-application step should enforce `must_not_include` and `must_include` body constraints before marking a correction as applied and advancing the manuscript to the standards gate. Currently the standards gate receives a manuscript that already declares its correction complete, and it appears to accept that declaration rather than re-scanning the body against the correction's constraint arrays. A post-correction body scan that compares raw body text against every active correction's `must_not_include` list should block advancement if any disallowed string is still present. This would have caught the six stale URLs before the standards pass and prevented `publishable: true` from being set on a body that violates its own correction record.

